Z||000012||Dave Gunsul||08-23-2001||05:36 PM||||I still can't believe people want power steering on their Fieros.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>A real sports car shouldn't have it anyway.<BR>To each his own I guess though. &lt;shrugs&gt;<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||13
Z||000013||mrfixit58||08-23-2001||05:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>Why do you think that drag racers all go with manual stering?  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Everybody in NASCAR uses them as does ARCA and most round-d-rounds racers. Think maybe it's the difference between between 5 to 10 seconds (NHRA) versus 2 to 4 hours behind the wheel? Personally, who cares how much power they suck up when your trying to parallel park :P .<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||6
Z||000014||Kasdan||08-23-2001||08:26 PM||||you people act like i pur it on there, it was there when i got the car, thats why i got it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, Holley Ram-Air, Power Steering, K&N Filtercharger, Bosch Wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, Konrad Distribuiter Cap/Rotor, New GT Seats, New Headliner, New Carpet, Ocelet Exhaust, Red Underbody Light Kit (coming soon)<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000015||Nashco||08-24-2001||12:30 AM||||I have a '69 VW, and the manual steering on it was much easier than my 88 GT.  My VW was modified, however; it had a baja kit that used a complete fiberglass front end (probably 70 pounds less?) and bigger tires (235/75s...little more weight to push around, but wouldn't effect stopped steering too much, only wider contact patch dragging it would).  I took that car in the sand dunes, and it drove in the dunes at low speeds like my fiero.  Don't get me wrong, I love a good feel on the wheel like my Fiero...just saying that my VW was much easier to turn.  I always wanted to put an aftermarket rack on it so that it would be easier to drive in the dunes...you wouldn't imagine how tired your arms can get after several hours of driving in the dunes with a manual shifting/steering car <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I think the power steering would be a great mod to spread on the net, because a LOT of people have a hard time with the steering at low speeds, especially with 84-87 cars.  This would be a very easy mod, especially if somebody made a bracket, pulley, and parts kit.  I know there are many people who would jump at the opportunity for such a ride-improving add on.  Remember that a lot of Fiero owners don't drive their car for racing excitement, but rather, as a daily driver.  This would be a *sweet* mod for somebody doing lots of city driving.<P>Keep us updated, we're all starving for pics!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||falconhulk||08-24-2001||08:21 AM||||Nascar uses it becasue they drive all day...thats corerect and it does not hurt that they are limited in the power they can generate anyway.  They also have way more power then the fiero has to spare.  The manual stering in the fiero is fantastic in my opnion, except at lower speeds trying to make tight turns.  I would gladly trade this for more HP.  Not trying to argue just saying that manual stering was a great choice for this car by Pontiac.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfixit58||08-24-2001||08:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>Nascar uses it becasue they drive all day...thats corerect and it does not hurt that they are limited in the power they can generate anyway. They also have way more power then the fiero has to spare.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, NASCAR only limits the motors cubic displacement and sometimes (on super speedways) restricts the intake size but there is no limit on how much power the motor builders are allowed to make. As Fiero owner and avid driver, I, personally, would gladly give up a few "ponys" for a little driving comfort. But, I agree it's not for everyone.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>The manual stering in the fiero is fantastic in my opnion, except at lower speeds trying to make tight turns.  I would gladly trade this for more HP.  Not trying to argue just saying that manual stering was a great choice for this car by Pontiac.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I agree that the steering in the Fiero is not uncomfortable. Especially at speeds. But parking lots can be difficult (for may wife  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ). And, I just really like options. So, if I can get power steering on the "cheap", I'd jump on it.<P>By the way, no argument intended, just a frendly discussion. If we all agreeed on everything, either everyone would be driving a Fiero or no one would!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||7
Z||000018||Kasdan||08-24-2001||08:59 AM||||ill try to steal *cough* a digi camera from school that saves images on a floppy so i can take them home and either post them on here or on a webpage for all of you to admire!  also will take all arouns pics of the car (engine, sound system, etc.)  The car wont be in the best condition, its kinda torn apart at the shop<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, Holley Ram-Air, Power Steering, K&N Filtercharger, Bosch Wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, Konrad Distribuiter Cap/Rotor, New GT Seats, New Headliner, New Carpet, Ocelet Exhaust, Red Underbody Light Kit (coming soon)<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
