Z||000020||falconhulk||08-24-2001||09:38 AM||||If I had a more powerful engine in it I would like to have PS also.  As it is now my wife will not drive it becasue she cannot steer it out of parking spaces.  Its not that big of a deal for me, except for the fact that she wants me to drive her everywhere in this car now!  I read Nascar put limiters on the engines this year to keep the groupings closer.  Thats why they were so close when Eardhart (not sure how to speel his name) died.  They figured it would make a more exciting race.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||mrfixit58||08-24-2001||11:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B> I read Nascar put limiters on the engines this year to keep the groupings closer.  Thats why they were so close when Eardhart (not sure how to speel his name) died.  They figured it would make a more exciting race.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your probably refering to the restrictor plates on the motor below the carb and/or the roof fin that runs across the top of the car just behind the windshield. It's purpose is to disrupt the air flow over the cars creating drag.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula88||08-24-2001||11:26 AM||||Actually, the restrictor plate, etc., are put on by NASCAR to make the race safer, not more exciting. It's primarily to reduce the top speed of the cars on super-speedway tracks like Daytona.  Without the restrictor plates, we could be seeing speeds upwards of 250 mph!  They've been hovering around the 190-200 mark for years, and the restrictor plates help to keep them there.||||reg||1
Z||000023||DRH||08-24-2001||11:28 AM||||falconhulk,<P>20HP (or even 10) is totally unrealistic. If it took that much power to just help turn the wheels you'd never be able to turn it manually. Not to mention the pump is more or less idling when the wheels aren't being turned anyway. Where did you get that number?||||reg||1
Z||000024||falconhulk||08-24-2001||11:46 AM||||got that number from the track.  Its just an accepted numer.  A guy showed me one time this little demonstration.....he starts up his car and then he quickly turns the wheel back in forth.  You could hear the revs drop and the engine load slightly.  Just having the pump hooked up without even turning the wheel robs horse power.  It does vary by the pump used and the car.  Granted the top end is for older cars like My 78 Cobra II.  But the lower end is definitely in the area of today.  10 HP or a little lower with lightwieght pulleys.  Remember people unhook thier AC pump for the same reason but your AC does not rob as much as PS.  If you have a better range what do you think it is?  I am not saying that my answer is the definitive but just what I have encountered as what most people go by.  ||||reg||1
Z||000025||falconhulk||08-24-2001||11:48 AM||||No Dale Earnhart (spelling) and other racers actually requested the restrictors to make the race closer.  I read that a while back and it was even mentioned during the race.  It was to keep the race exciting.  But my point was the cars are capable of making more HP then they need so PS does not mean a thing to them.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mickey_Moose||08-24-2001||11:51 AM||||You know, if you want the power steering and don't want to lose any hp, you can always set it up using and electric motor to drive the pump and accessories. I have seen this done on many cars that people use for bracket racing (the only thing that runs off the engine is the alternator - I have even seen a could that had electric water pumps)<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000027||falconhulk||08-24-2001||01:51 PM||||yep a lot of people go to the electric fan and water pump so save HP.  Most go to manual rack, I have never seen the electric PS though.  Might be interesting.  Here is the down side to that electric stuff people dont know:  The more current you draw through the alternator the harder the engine has to work to turn the alternator. This is a function of the magnetic field in the windings.  You definitely save over direct drive acc.  but I would be interested to know how much!  You know a lot of drag racers even disconnect the alternator on thier runs and let the spark come from the battery.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000028||mrfixit58||08-24-2001||03:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>No Dale Earnhart (spelling) and other racers actually requested the restrictors to make the race closer.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree! Anyone who knows anything about NASCAR knows that Earnhart HATED restrictor plate racing... even though he was one of the best at it. Now, you may have read something from some other racer but not from Dale.<P>Remember, there are around 3000 members on this message board. If you make a statement, be aware that you may be challenged to back it up. I'm not trying to flame you, just giving a frendly reminder.<P>Sorry for getting off topic,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||falconhulk||08-24-2001||03:14 PM||||hey I will tell you I know absolutely nothing about NASCAR!  I just happened to be watching the last lap that day.  I swear to go they said that.  Why would I make that up?  The restrictor plates I think you are refering to have been in place for a few years right?  Well what I am talking about was just done this year.  It absolutely was to keep people from runnign away with the race.  I have a feeling we are talking about 2 diffrent things.  Once again I defer to your knowlegde of NASCAR but am just spitting out what I heard from the TV and read in an article after the crash.||||reg||1
Z||000030||falconhulk||08-24-2001||03:19 PM||||mrfixit58<BR>Check you Private Messages!||||reg||1
Z||000031||falconhulk||08-24-2001||03:22 PM||||Up until the last minute, drivers and crews were scurrying to make adjustments to their car setups. No one is really sure how this race will pan out, given the recent required changes to the cars. NASCAR President Ray Evernham was instrumental in mandating new changes to the engine and aerodynamic configurations for the super speedway tracks. Gone this year are the restrictor plates from the intake manifolds. The car bodies have been modified to include higher front-end skirts and steeper-angled rear spoilers. These changes, combined with the new rev limiters, seemed to function well last year when they were experimented with at Daytona. The Daytona race produced the results that drivers and fans were looking for: better throttle response with the new fuel injectors; lower top-end speeds and reduced bunching of cars. Depending on the results of the New Orleans race, NASCAR may implement these modifications on other tracks such as Atlanta and Texas later this year. <BR>Read this site: <A HREF="http://www.autoracing1.com/GoodBoys/2001/0531future.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autoracing1.com/GoodBoys/2001/0531future.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000032||mrfixit58||08-24-2001||03:33 PM||||I'm at work and can't check my home e-mail right now but, as far as the URL:<P>He, he, he.... good artical. Of corse you do realize that's it's written as tongue-n-cheek.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000033||falconhulk||08-24-2001||03:36 PM||||Yes but the rev limiter were and are real.  Thats all I am trying to say.  See?  I think you misunderstood? Also Private Messages are a new function of this fourm.  Go out to the main page and go to the bottom you will see that you have new messages.  I realize I may have not used the correct terms to describe what I meant but I was not lying.  When that article says the "rev limiters that were tried out in Daytona"  That is the truth.  The rest is conjecture.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||mrfixit58||08-24-2001||04:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>Yes but the rev limiter were and are real.  ...  When that article says the "rev limiters that were tried out in Daytona"  That is the truth.  The rest is conjecture.<P>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 08-24-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yes, all cars have rev limiters. But, they are put into the ignition system to keep the motor from over reving. The ignition box sits close to the driver and he can actually pull the limiter out any time he choses. This is usually done at the end of the race to get a few more ponys out of the motor. They are placed there by the builder and are not mandated by NASCAR. As far as the artical... it's all in good fun.<P>I'll have to check the private mail thing.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000035||Pontiaddict||08-24-2001||04:46 PM||||Not only that, but they have redudnant ignition systems on the car in case they have problems with one, they can switch over to the other at any time. Way off topic now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000036||Ferrari_cdn||08-24-2001||06:15 PM||||I have never driven a car without power steering so I don't know how hard it is.  I didn't read the whole thread here, but how difficult is it?||||reg||1
Z||000037||Ryan Wright||08-24-2001||07:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>A real sports car shouldn't have it anyway.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dave, I feel the same way.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>because a LOT of people have a hard time with the steering at low speeds, especially with 84-87 cars. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's just fine with me. Keeps my wife from wanting to drive the car. "It's too hard to turn." ... Exactly! <BR><P>------------------<BR>-Ryan Wright (http://www.ryanwright.com)||||reg||1
Z||000038||Kasdan||08-25-2001||12:41 AM||||im a spoiled 17 year old, i like p/s :P||||reg||1
Z||000039||Primaris||08-25-2001||12:57 AM||||On pre '88's get rid of the steering damper.  Your steering will lighten up a lot.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000040||mrfiero||08-25-2001||12:58 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B> NASCAR President Ray Evernham was instrumental in mandating new changes to the engine and aerodynamic configurations for the super speedway tracks.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>???  Ray Evernham is a crew chief not the president of NASCAR.  Bill France Jr is the prez.<P>Ray used to be Jeff Gordons crew chief, but I think he left to head up Dodge's retirn to NASCAR.  I'm not sure what driver he's with though.<P>I just checked and he is Bill Elliot's crew chief.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Nashco||09-21-2001||12:14 PM||||Kasdan, whatever happened with this?  Are you still around?  What ever happened with getting pics?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000042||Formula88||09-21-2001||12:48 PM||||I can see a need for power steering on a Fiero.  Not stock, but if you've modded the suspension and put serious meat on the front, you're gonna want some power steering to turn all that extra rubber.<P>As for the power steering argument about turning the wheel parked, that's totally unrealistic.  Sitting still requires the most possible effort to turn the wheel possible.  If you're using that as your argument, it only works if you're racing while stopped.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>You can tell this just by getting in your Fiero.  How much harder is it to turn the wheel stopped vs. driving at 60mph?  Equally, the power steering pump will put a much higher drain on power stopped than at 60mph.  This is just one illustration, but since most races involve the cars moving, the amount of power it draws while stopped isn't critical.||||reg||6
Z||000043||Wannabe||09-21-2001||03:26 PM||||The biggest benefit of power steering would be using a faster steering gear in the rack so that fewer turns are required lock to lock. The stock manual steering on the '88's is actually a bit to light for my taste, but could definitely stand to be quicker, like about 2-2.5 turns as opposed to 3.1||||reg||1
Z||000044||Nashco||09-21-2001||06:17 PM||||You want FEWER turns?  That would make it even harder.  I think how it is stock is just about right...effort at a stop, but not too hard to overcome, and ease while driving.  Any fewer turns, two for example, would make turning while parked really tough and turning while at low speeds feel like you're parked.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000045||Wannabe||09-21-2001||09:13 PM||||Of course it would make it harder - that's where the power steering comes in. There's not much point in adding power steering with the stock ratio as it's too light to begin with, but if you add power steering and go to a quicker ratio, you can have the best of both worlds. The only significant drawbacks would be some loss of steering feedback and possibly too much sensitivity at high speeds. ||||reg||1
Z||000046||Nashco||09-22-2001||02:19 AM||||Oh, my bad Wannabe, I misread your post.  I agree, with power steering, I would want to bump up the ratio.  But...most people would like to leave it stock, so it adds ease to the steering effort.  That's why so many people want power steering is because they don't like how hard it is to steer.  I, personally, would rather have a quicker responding wheel than more ease.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
